course: books
lesson: 79
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 5: The Elephant's Child, part 1 (What does the Crocodile have for dinner?)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Long ago elephants had no trunk, only a bulgy nose as big as a boot, and one young Elephant's Child was so full of "'satiable curtiosity" (endless curiosity) that he asked everybody questions, and all his aunts and uncles spanked him for it.
explain: One morning he asks the one question nobody dares to answer, "What does the Crocodile have for dinner?", and the Kolokolo Bird tells him to go and find out at the great grey-green, greasy Limpopo River.
explain: He packs bananas, sugar-cane and melons, says goodbye, and walks across Africa, dropping melon rind all the way because he cannot pick anything up with his little nose.
explain: At the river a Python-Rock-Snake spanks him too, and then he steps on a "log" that is really the Crocodile, which winks, weeps fake tears and says "Come hither, Little One... I'll whisper."
explain: The trusting Elephant's Child leans close, and the Crocodile grabs him by the nose and says he will begin his dinner with Elephant's Child: the part ends with a funny squashed-nose cry for help and a real danger.
fact: "In the High and Far-Off Times the Elephant ... had no trunk", only "a blackish, bulgy nose, as big as a boot" that he could wriggle but not pick things up with.
fact: "'Satiable curtiosity" is Kipling's baby-talk for "insatiable curiosity", wanting to know things so much that you are never satisfied.
fact: He asked his tall aunt the Ostrich about her tail-feathers, his tall uncle the Giraffe why his skin was spotty, his broad aunt the Hippopotamus why her eyes were red, and his hairy uncle the Baboon why melons tasted just so; each one spanked him.
fact: In old stories grown-ups spank children all the time; today we know that hitting children is wrong, and Kipling makes it silly on purpose.
fact: His new question, "What does the Crocodile have for dinner?", made everybody say "Hush!" "in a loud and dretful tone" ("dretful" = dreadful).
fact: The Kolokolo Bird, sitting in the middle of a wait-a-bit thorn-bush (a thorny African bush that catches at you), said: "Go to the banks of the great grey-green, greasy Limpopo River, all set about with fever-trees, and find out."
fact: He took "a hundred pounds of bananas (the little short red kind)", "a hundred pounds of sugar-cane (the long purple kind)", and "seventeen melons (the greeny-crackly kind)".
fact: His families "spanked him once more for luck", and he went off "a little warm, but not at all astonished", "throwing the rind about, because he could not pick it up".
fact: He went from Graham's Town to Kimberley and to Khama's Country (real places in South Africa and Botswana), and till that day he "had never seen a Crocodile, and did not know what one was like".
fact: The "Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snake" answered "in a voice of dretful scorn", "Have I seen a Crocodile? What will you ask me next?", and spanked him "with his scalesome, flailsome tail"; the Elephant's Child, who was always most polite and began his questions with "'Scuse me", said "I suppose this is the same thing" (another spanking for asking) and politely helped coil him up on his rock again.
fact: He trod on "what he thought was a log of wood", but it was the Crocodile, who "winked one eye--like this!", then winked the other eye and lifted half his tail out of the mud, and "wept crocodile-tears to show it was quite true".
fact: "Crocodile tears" means pretend sadness; real crocodiles do make tears, but only to keep their eyes wet, not because they feel sorry.
fact: The Crocodile caught him "by his little nose" and said "between his teeth" that "to-day I will begin with Elephant's Child!"; with his nose squeezed, the Elephant's Child cried "Led go! You are hurtig be!" (Let go! You are hurting me!).
fact: Real places and creatures: the Limpopo is a real river in southern Africa; fever trees are real yellow-barked trees of wet ground, named long ago because people near them caught fever (malaria) from mosquitoes; the African rock python is Africa's biggest snake.
fact: Real biology: Nile crocodiles really do lie still like logs at the water's edge and ambush animals that come to drink, even big ones; that is why going close to a crocodile is truly dangerous.
fact: The "Precession of the Equinoxes" is a real, very slow wobble of the Earth that takes about 26,000 years, so saying something happened "in the middle of" it one morning is Kipling's joke.
exercise: What did elephants have instead of a trunk long ago, in the story? | A blackish, bulgy nose as big as a boot | A short pink snout, just like a pig's snout | A long beak like a bird's | No nose at all
exercise: What could the Elephant's Child NOT do with his old nose? | Pick things up | Wriggle it about | Smell things with it | Breathe in and out
exercise: What does "'satiable curtiosity" mean? | Never-ending curiosity | A big appetite | Very good manners | A terribly bad temper
exercise: What did the Elephant's Child ask his tall aunt, the Ostrich? | Why her tail-feathers grew just so | Why her eyes were so very red | Why her skin was all spotty | Why melons tasted the way they did
exercise: Whom did he ask why his skin was spotty? | His tall uncle, the Giraffe | His hairy uncle, the Baboon | His broad aunt, the Hippopotamus | His tall aunt, the Ostrich
exercise: What did he ask his hairy uncle, the Baboon? | Why melons tasted just so | Why his tail was so long | Where the Limpopo River was | What a Crocodile looked like
exercise: What did everyone do when he asked a question? | Spanked him | Answered him | Laughed at him | Just ignored him
exercise: What new question did he ask one fine morning? | "What does the Crocodile have for dinner?" | "Why do the Ostrich's feathers grow so?" | "Where is the great grey-green river?" | "How did the Camel get his big hump?"
exercise: What did everybody say to his new question? | "Hush!" | "Humph!" | "How very rude!" | "Go away!"
exercise: Who told him where to find out? | The Kolokolo Bird | The Python-Rock-Snake | His tall aunt, the Ostrich | Baviaan the Baboon
exercise: Where was the Kolokolo Bird sitting? | In the middle of a wait-a-bit thorn-bush | On the top of a palm tree by the Red Sea | On the back of the broad Hippopotamus | On a rock by the Limpopo River
exercise: How does the story describe the Limpopo River? | Great grey-green, greasy, all set about with fever-trees | Wide, blue and sparkling, all set about with palm trees | Small, clear and cold, running through the High Veldt | Salty and calm, lying beside an uninhabited island
exercise: Which food did he NOT take on his journey? | Cake and currants | Bananas | Long purple sugar-cane | Melons
exercise: How many melons did he take? | Seventeen | About a hundred | Just seven | Seventy-seven
exercise: What kind of bananas did he take? | The little short red kind | The long, curly yellow kind | The green unripe kind | The big purple kind
exercise: Why did he throw the melon rind about? | He could not pick it up with his nose | He wanted to leave a trail to find home | He was angry with all his relations | He thought the birds would like to eat it
exercise: How did he feel as he set off after the last spanking? | A little warm, but not at all astonished | Very sad, and crying all along the road | Furious, and shouting at all his family | Afraid, and wanting to turn back home
exercise: Had he ever seen a Crocodile before? | No, never | Yes, just once | Yes, often | Only in a book
exercise: What was the first creature he found by the river? | A Bi-Coloured-Python-Rock-Snake | The Crocodile, winking one eye | A broad Hippopotamus in the mud | The Kolokolo Bird in a thorn-bush
exercise: What did the Python do when asked what the Crocodile has for dinner? | Spanked him with his scalesome, flailsome tail | Told him the answer in a quiet little whisper at once | Swallowed him whole like a Whale swallows | Ran away and hid under a fever-tree root
exercise: What did the Elephant's Child do for the Python after being spanked? | Helped to coil him up on the rock again | Spanked him back hard with his little nose | Gave him seventeen of his melons | Told the Kolokolo Bird about him
exercise: What did he think the Crocodile was when he trod on it? | A log of wood | A big grey rock | A sleeping snake | A pile of leaves
exercise: What did the Crocodile do to show he really was the Crocodile? | Wept crocodile-tears | Winked both eyes at once | Opened his mouth | Swished his tail
exercise: What does "crocodile tears" mean today? | Pretend sadness | Happy tears | Real, deep grief | Tears of pain
exercise: What did the Crocodile say he would whisper? | What he had for dinner | Where the river began | How to grow a long trunk | Who the Kolokolo was
exercise: What did "Led go! You are hurtig be!" really mean? | Let go! You are hurting me! | Lead on! You are helping me! | Let's go! You are hurrying me! | Leave now! You are hugging me!
exercise: Real biology: how do real crocodiles often catch animals? | They lie still like logs and ambush animals that come to drink | They chase them for miles across the open grassland at noon | They climb trees and drop down on animals that walk under them | They call the animals over with a friendly, gentle whisper
exercise: Thinking back to the Leopard story: what is Baviaan? | A dog-headed, barking Baboon | A tall Giraffe with blotches | A small clever fish | A Djinn in a dust-cloud
test: Ethan says: "The Elephant's Child was a naughty kid who never said please." Put him right. | He was always most polite, saying "'Scuse me" | He was rude and shouted at all his relations | He stole a cake from a Parsee on the beach | He was idle and said "Humph!" to everyone
test: Molly, Gen Alpha: "Crocodile was like 'come here bestie, I'll whisper', sus much?" ("sus" = suspicious.) Why should the Elephant's Child have been careful? | Crocodiles catch animals at the water's edge | Crocodiles tell secrets to the Kolokolo Bird | Crocodiles never whisper, they always shout | Crocodiles only eat bananas and sugar-cane
test: What would have happened if the Elephant's Child had not leaned close to hear the whisper? | The Crocodile could not have caught his nose | The Crocodile would have told him the answer kindly | He would have grown a trunk from waiting anyway | The Python would have spanked him even harder
test: True or false: the Elephant's Child knew what a Crocodile looked like before his trip. | False: he had never seen one and did not know | True: his mother had shown him a picture | True: he had met one at the Limpopo before | False: but the Python showed him at once
test: Nana, Kerry: "Ah, the poor craythur, sure he only wanted to know." ("craythur" = creature.) Why did everybody say "Hush!" to his question? | It was a scary, dangerous question to ask | Nobody had ever heard of the Crocodile | The question was asked during dinner-time | The Kolokolo Bird was asleep in the bush
test: Big bro, rough: "Seventeen melons, a hundred pounds of bananas? He's a proper greedy guts, that one." Why did he take so much food? | It was a long journey across Africa | He wanted to feed the Crocodile | His mother told him to share it | He was going to sell it at market
test: The Elephant's Child asks the Python if he has seen a Crocodile. What does the scornful Python answer, after "Have I seen a Crocodile?" | "What will you ask me next?" | "Some people do not know what is good for them" | "Come hither, Little One" | "Them that takes cakes makes dreadful mistakes"
test: Gen Z cousin: "Elephant's Child really said 'I suppose this is the same thing' after the snake spanked him, he's so unbothered." What "same thing" did he mean? | Being spanked for his curiosity | Being lost in a strange land | Being hungry after his walk | Being tricked by a clever fish
test: The Leopard story had a puzzle-word, "spots". What is Kipling's word game in "satiable curtiosity"? | Baby-talk for "insatiable curiosity" | A real word for a kind of melon | A secret name of the Kolokolo Bird | The name of a river in Africa
test: The Mariner in the Whale story was "a man of infinite-resource-and-sagacity". Which is true of the Elephant's Child so far? | He is curious and polite but too trusting | He is clever and full of tricks like the Mariner | He is idle and rude like the Camel | He is greedy and has few manners like the Rhino
test: In the Camel story, the Horse, Dog and Ox each came and spoke to the Camel in turn. Who comes to the Elephant's Child in turn with spanks? | His aunts and uncles, one after another | The Horse, the Dog and the Ox, one by one | The Leopard and the hunter, side by side | The Whale and the 'Stute Fish, together
test: Sipho, South Africa: "The Limpopo? That's lekker far from Graham's Town, hey." ("lekker" = really, very.) Which way did the Elephant's Child walk? | From Graham's Town to Kimberley to Khama's Country | From Kimberley straight back down to Graham's Town | From the High Veldt into a shadowy forest | From the Red Sea across to the Howling Desert
test: Why is it funny that the Elephant's Child "helped to coil him up on the rock again" just after the Python spanked him? | He is so polite that he helps the one who hit him | He wanted to trap the Python under the rock | The Python had asked him for a melon in return | He was hoping to get spanked again for luck
test: ★★★ He took a hundred pounds of bananas and a hundred pounds of sugar-cane. How many pounds is that in all, not counting the melons? | Two hundred | One hundred | Two hundred and seventeen | One hundred and seventeen
test: ★★★ The Crocodile winked "one eye", then "the other eye". Read carefully: what did he do just after the second wink? | Lifted half his tail out of the mud | Wept crocodile-tears to prove it | Caught the Elephant's Child's nose | Said "I'll whisper" very quietly
test: ★★★ Spankers before the trip: his father, his mother, the Ostrich, the Giraffe, the Hippopotamus and the Baboon. Which of the four options is NOT one of his aunts or uncles? | His mother | The Ostrich | The Hippopotamus | The Baboon
exam: Identify the question which provoked the universal response of "Hush!" from the relations of the Elephant's Child. | What the Crocodile has for dinner | Why the Ostrich's feathers grow so | Why the Giraffe's skin is spotty | Why the Hippopotamus has red eyes
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Elephant's Child discarded the rind of the melons along his route. | He was unable to pick it up with his nose | He intended to mark the way home with it | He wished to provide food for the birds | He was instructed to do so by his mother
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "crocodile tears" in contemporary usage. | A display of sorrow which is not genuine | A display of joy upon receipt of news | A display of pain following an injury | A display of anger at an injustice
exam: State, in accordance with established zoology, the customary method of predation employed by the Nile crocodile. | Remaining motionless at the water's edge and ambushing animals that drink | Pursuing animals at speed across open grassland throughout the day | Descending upon animals from the branches of trees beside the river | Summoning animals by whispering to them in a quiet and friendly voice
